From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 04:44:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/exynos: Add the Exynos9820 DECON driver

DECON_F blends the output of the DPP windows and pushes it, optionally
through the DSC encoders, into DSIM. This covers the single window
command mode path the Galaxy S10 family uses: DPP0 into window 0, then
DSCC and the two DSC encoders, then OUTFIFO0 and OUTFIFO1, then
DSIM_IF0.

It is a component of the existing exynos_drm master rather than a driver
with a drm_device of its own: the SoC has one display pipeline, and a
second card would mean a second set of GEM and IOMMU state on the same
hardware.

The DSC parameters are not computed here. The panel already runs the
picture through the DSC core helpers, so this asks the same helpers for
the half of the picture one encoder sees, and keeps only what the core
has no field for: the component configuration word, the range parameter
selector and the width the encoder emits. Two copies of the VESA
arithmetic could drift apart while both still looked plausible.

Scanout is started on the first atomic flush rather than in
atomic_enable. drm_atomic_helper_commit_tail_rpm() runs
commit_modeset_enables() before commit_planes(), so at enable time the
bridge chain has not brought the link up and the panel is still in
reset; arming the block against the tearing effect signal there points
it at a peripheral that cannot answer. For the same reason the interrupt
is requested with IRQF_NO_AUTOEN and enabled only once the block is
powered and the pending bits the bootloader left are cleared: the line
is level triggered, so an unacknowledged source is not a lost interrupt
but a screaming one.

UNVERIFIED: initial_dec_delay.  The vendor writes 0x1b4 rather than the
computed delay because VESA_SCR_V4 is not defined in its build
(LineageOS/android_kernel_samsung_exynos9820, commit 7d08ec364a06,
drivers/video/fbdev/exynos/dpu20/cal_9820/decon_reg.c:1073), while the
PPS the panel receives carries the computed 616 for the same field. One
of the two ends is misconfigured on the vendor kernel as well, and only
a test pattern on the panel can say which.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
